## =====================================================================
## Nested action blocks - where you write a block is what it means
## =====================================================================
##
## An 'action ... end' block is a for-loop. What you write inside it runs
## once per iteration, so the nesting in the file IS the nesting of the
## scan. This file is the worked example of that; it needs no hardware
## beyond a PCIe-6351 and runs in a few seconds.
##
##   pscan --dry-run examples/nested_loops.con
##
## WHAT THIS ONE DOES
##
##   for each of 5 X positions          (ao0, 0..4 V)
##       for each of 3 Y positions      (ao1, 0..2 V)
##           take 4 traces and average them later
##
##   5 x 3 x 4 = 60 traces.
##
## >>> This drives real voltages out of ao0 and ao1. Check what is
## >>> connected before running it.
##
## WHY NESTING MATTERS: WHERE 'count' SITS
##
## Move the count block and you change the experiment, not just the
## order. Same three blocks, same 60 traces, three different meanings:
##
##   count INSIDE both axes      average each point
##                               -> noise at a point averages down;
##                                  slow drift does not
##
##   count around the Y axis     repeat each row
##                               -> a row takes 4x longer, and drift
##                                  within a row is still in the data
##
##   count OUTSIDE both axes     repeat the whole image 4 times
##                               -> each image is a snapshot, and the
##                                  four can be compared for drift
##                                  instead of being averaged blindly
##
## The third is impossible to say with a global count, which is what
## 'action count' used to be. This is the reason for the whole change.
##
## FLAT FILES STILL WORK, UNCHANGED
##
## A config that does not nest is read exactly as it always was: axes
## nest by axis id, lowest outermost, and count applies at each
## coordinate. examples/bringup_6351.con is one. Nothing written before
## nesting existed means anything different now, and there is nothing to
## migrate.
##
## THE RULES
##
##   - Loops are the axis blocks and 'count'. Only they may contain
##     other blocks.
##   - A loop must contain something.
##   - Every recording block (a2d, scope, webcam, ixon, tucsen) must sit
##     at the same depth, so every .d file from one run has the same
##     number of traces.
##   - In a nested file the 'axis' number no longer decides the order -
##     the nesting does. It still names the axis in the log and is what
##     'restore' belongs to.

## --- X: the outer axis. Steps once per complete Y sweep. ------------
action d2a
axis 97
backend daqmx
device Dev1
channel 0
units volts
scan 0.0 4.0 1.0
limits 0.0 10.0
settle 0.01
restore

    ## --- Y: the inner axis. Sweeps fully at every X. ----------------
    action d2a
    axis 98
    backend daqmx
    device Dev1
    channel 1
    units volts
    scan 0.0 2.0 1.0
    limits 0.0 10.0
    settle 0.01
    restore

        ## --- 4 traces at each (X, Y), to average later. -------------
        ##
        ## Written here, inside both axes, this averages a POINT. Move
        ## this block outside the Y loop and it repeats a ROW; outside
        ## the X loop and it repeats the whole IMAGE.
        action count
        count 4

            action a2d
            channel 0
            backend daqmx
            device Dev1
            v_min -1.0
            v_max 1.0
            terminal rse
            sample_rate 10000
            n_samples 1000
            end

        end
    end
end
